<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 4.1 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-differentiation-ii" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Differentiation II</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Grade <m>11</m> you learned that the derivative of a function gives the gradient of its curve at any point, and you used it to find equations of tangents and normals. This chapter uses the derivative to study the shape of a curve.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will find the stationary points of a curve, where the gradient is zero, and decide whether each one is a maximum, a minimum or a point of inflection. You will use stationary points and intercepts to sketch curves. You will then apply differentiation to problems about greatest and least values, and to kinematics, where it links displacement, velocity and acceleration.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finding a maximum or a minimum is how a business finds its greatest profit, and how an engineer designs a water tank that uses the least material.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- include sections -->
  <xi:include href="sec-stationary-points-and-their-nature.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-curve-sketching.ptx" />
  <xi:include href="sec-application-of-differentiation.ptx" />

</chapter>
